@meta
id: 15-02
title: The anchor decides who goes
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-delete
par: 22
minutes: 5
vscode: search for //, look at each result, decide, delete, repeat
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
A colleague debugged `OrderProvider` by commenting things out instead of
deleting them, and the review has asked for the corpse to be removed.
Every line that is nothing but commented-out code goes.

The trap is that the file also has real comments, the short ones at the
end of a line of live code. Those stay, and so does the code in front of
them. A pattern that just says "contains two slashes" would take the
live lines with the dead ones.

The `:g` pattern is a full regex, the same dialect you used in chapter
13. The thing that separates a dead line from a live one here is not
what it contains but where the slashes sit: at the start of the line,
after nothing but indentation. Say that, and only the dead lines match.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/d` | delete every line matching `pattern` |
| `^` | in a pattern, the start of the line |
| `\s*` | any amount of whitespace |
| `\/` | a literal slash inside a slash-delimited pattern |

@hints
Look at what a dead line and a live line have in common, then at what
they do not. The slashes are on both. Their position is not.
--
Anchor the pattern to the start of the line, allow for the indentation,
then the two slashes. Each slash needs a backslash in front of it,
because a bare slash would end the pattern early.
--
`:g/^\s*\/\//d` and Enter. Nine dead lines go; the four lines with a
trailing comment do not move.

@solution
    :g/^\s*\/\//d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

The `^` is the whole lesson. Without it the pattern matches thirteen
lines and deletes four lines of working code. With it, nine. Whenever
you reach for `:g` with a `d` after it, the question to ask is not "what
do the lines I want contain" but "what do the lines I do not want
contain that mine do not". Anchors, `^` and `$`, are usually the answer.

The backslashes in `\/\/` are unlovely. Vim lets you use almost any
punctuation as the delimiter instead of `/`, so `:g#^\s*//#d` is the
same command with nothing to escape. Worth knowing for patterns full of
slashes, like paths and URLs.

And as before: `:g/^\s*\/\/` with no `d` prints the candidates first.
For a destructive command on a file you did not write, that preview is
the professional habit.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
// import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    // @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
    // console.log('findOne', id, order);

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND'); // 404, not sentry
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    // const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: order.customerId } });

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED'); // finance asked for 409 here
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
    // await this.emailService.send(customer.email, 'order-cancelled', { order });
    // console.log('refund done', order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' }, // reason is optional
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    // if (order.status === 'CANCELLED') {
    //   throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED');
    // }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' }, // shippedAt is set by the trigger
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND'); // 404, not sentry
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED'); // finance asked for 409 here
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' }, // reason is optional
    });
  }

  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' }, // shippedAt is set by the trigger
    });
  }
}
